 use and any memory content.\nL646: ### FAQ\nL647: \nL648: ### How does ChatGPT’s pricing work?\nL649: \nL650: The free version of ChatGPT is available to everyone. Paid plans (Go, Plus, Business, and Enterprise) are priced per user per month. We offer monthly plans for Go, Plus and Business and annual plans for Business and Enterprise.\nL651: \nL652: ### Is ChatGPT free to use?\nL653: \nL654: The free version of ChatGPT is available to everyone. Upgrading to Go, Plus, Business, or Enterprise offers a more powerful experience through more access to additional models and features.\nL655: ### Does OpenAI offer a ChatGPT plan for educational institutions?\nL656: \nL657: Yes. We offer cite65†ChatGPT for Teachers⁠ , a free plan for verified U.S. K–12 educators through June 2027, and cite52†ChatGPT Edu⁠ , an affordable plan that lets universities deploy AI across their campus communities. Both plans include enhanced security and privacy controls, with additional administrative features available for schools, districts, and universities. cite39†Contact our team⁠ to learn more.\nL658: ### Does OpenAI offer a discount for nonprofits?\nL659: \nL660: Yes. Through OpenAI for Nonprofits, nonprofits can now access up to a 75% discount on ChatGPT Business or ChatGPT Enterprise. cite66†Contact our sales team⁠ to get started.\nL661: \nL662: ### How many users are supported on each subscription plan?\nL663: \nL664: Go, Free, and Plus plans are designed to be used by individuals. Business and Enterprise are for businesses. Business plans are available starting at 2 users. Please cite39†contact sales⁠ to learn more about our Enterprise plan.\nL665: ### What are my payment options?\nL666: \nL667: You can purchase ChatGPT Go, Plus, Pro, or Business with any major credit card. For ChatGPT Enterprise, please cite39†contact sales⁠ for alternative payment options such as invoicing.\nL668: ### How secure is ChatGPT?\nL669: All data is encrypted in transit (TLS 1.2). Data at rest is encrypted at rest (AES-256), and strict access controls are used to limit who can access data. Our security team has an on-call rotation that has 24/7/365 coverage and is paged in case of any potential security incident. We offer a cite67†Bug Bounty Program⁠†openai.com for responsible disclosure of vulnerabilities discovered on our platform and products. Please visit our cite68†Trust Portal⁠†trust.openai.com for more details.\nL670: ### How does ChatGPT use my data?\nL671: \nL672: We give you control over your data. Read more about cite69†how your data is used⁠†openai.com and our cite70†Enterprise privacy⁠†openai.com .\nL673: \nL674: OpenAI\nL675: \nL676:   * cite71†Research†openai.com L677:   * cite72†Safety†openai.com L678:   * cite73†API†openai.com L679:   * cite74†News†openai.com L680: \nL681: Terms & Policies\nL682: \nL683:   * cite75†Terms of Use†openai.com L684:   * cite76†Privacy Policy†openai.com L685:   * cite77†Usage Policy†openai.com L686:   * cite78†Other policies†openai.com L687: \nL688: OpenAI © 2015–2026 Manage cookies\nL689: \nL690: English United States\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2304.03442] Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior (https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442)\nciteturn16view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Human-Computer Interaction\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 7 Apr 2023 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 6 Aug 2023 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Joon Sung Park , cite7†Joseph C. O'Brien , cite8†Carrie J. Cai , cite9†Meredith Ringel Morris , cite10†Percy Liang , cite11†Michael S. Bernstein L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior, by Joon Sung Park and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Believable proxies of human behavior can empower interactive applications ranging from immersive environments to rehearsal spaces for interpersonal communication to prototyping tools. In this paper, we introduce generative agents--computational software agents that simulate believable human behavior.\nL17: Generative agents wake up, cook breakfast, and head to work; artists paint, while authors write; they form opinions, notice each other, and initiate conversations; they remember and reflect on days past as they plan the next day.\nL18: To enable generative agents, we describe an architecture that extends a large language model to store a complete record of the agent's experiences using natural language, synthesize those memories over time into higher-level reflections, and retrieve them dynamically to plan behavior. We instantiate generative agents to populate an interactive sandbox environment inspired by The Sims, where end users can interact with a small town of twenty five agents using natural language.\nL19: In an evaluation, these generative agents produce believable individual and emergent social behaviors: for example, starting with only a single user-specified notion that one agent wants to throw a Valentine's Day party, the agents autonomously spread invitations to the party over the next two days, make new acquaintances, ask each other out on dates to the party, and coordinate to show up for the party together at the right time.\nL20: We demonstrate through ablation that the components of our agent architecture--observation, planning, and reflection--each contribute critically to the believability of agent behavior. By fusing large language models with computational, interactive agents, this work introduces architectural and interaction patterns for enabling believable simulations of human behavior.\nL21: Subjects:  | Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2304.03442 [cs.HC]\nL23:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2304.03442v2 [cs.HC] for this version)\nL24:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2304.03442†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Joon Sung Park [cite17†view email ]\nL28: cite5†[v1] Fri, 7 Apr 2023 01:55:19 UTC (34,149 KB)\nL29: [v2] Sun, 6 Aug 2023 00:21:19 UTC (12,955 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior, by Joon Sung Park and 5 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite12†View PDF L38:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite18†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite19†view license "
